  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather today through late this\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAn extended period of dry weather is expected through the end \nof the week under extremely dry airmass. Noreaster spins off of\nthe mid-Atlantic coast before exiting to the east Thu into Fri.\nSome discrepancies in model guidance are beginning to show with\na few ensemble solutions closing off an upper low over the \nnortheast by the end of the week which could bring some light \nrain/snow showers to the area Thursday night into Friday. Thus, \nconfidence is lower in no precipitation expected through the end\nof the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Nor'easter could potentially cause minor beach \nerosion to the coast from tonight into possibly Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cutoff low off the Mid Atlantic Coast lifts Wednesday morning\nnortheast through Thursday morning, then either continues \nnortheast into The Maritimes or heads eastward Friday.\n\nEnsemble guidance generally keeps the storm well offshore but a\nfew solutions bring it further north towards our area. If the \nstronger, more northern solutions for this Nor'easter are \ncorrect, then there could be some minor beach erosion along \nportions of the Downeast coast around the time of high tide\ntonight into at least Thursday. While predicted water levels \nwill remain below coastal flood benchmarks, there could be \nsufficient wave setup/runup over multiple high tide cycles to \ncause some minor beach erosion at more vulnerable locations \nalong the coasts of Hancock and Washington Counties. With the \nvast majority of solutions with the Nor'easter not favoring a \nstronger and more northward track at this time, the current \nchance for even minor beach erosion with this system is very \nlow, at 10-20%.",
